\chapter{Structured diagnostics}
\label{c:sdiag}\cutname{sdiag.html}

Both the bytecode and native compiler can emit diagnostic under
a structured format by using the "-log-format <format>" flag.
Supported formats are currently
\begin{itemize}
\item json
\item s-expression
\end{itemize}

The flag "-log-file <path>" determine where the diagnostics are stored if
present. Otherwise, the structured diagnostic are displayed on the terminal
standard output.

\section{s:sdiag_versioning}{Versioning policy and subtyping}

In order to make the compiler diagnostics as easy to use as possible by
independent tools, The compiler and its tools enforce a strict versioning
and evolution policy.

This policy is a data-oriented version of semantic versioning for algebraic data
types:

\begin{itemize}
\item minor updates can refine a schema into a subtype of the previous schema
\item major updates can delete deprecated fields from record type or add new
  constructors to sum type.
\end{itemize}


With this policy, we can ensure that a compiler which is internally building a
diagnostic for the version "major.minor" of a schema can always externally
output a structured diagnostic for any version "major.prev" where "prev" $\le$
"minor" by coercing the diagnostic at version "major.minor" into its subtype at
version "major.prev".

The version of the schema used can be controlled with the "-log-version" flag.
In the absence of such flags, the compiler emits structured diagnostic in a
format as downward compatible as possible.

\subsection{ss:sdiag-variant-subtyping}{Annotated versioned data types}

A good way to visualise the evolution of diagnostic data types across versions is
to represent them as usual algebraic data types but annotated with versioning and
evolution metadata. During the life cycle of a specific versioned data types, a
given variant constructor or record field may go through the following stages:

\begin{enumerate}
\item preview "[\@preview M.m Parent]",
  a constructor can be derived from a "Parent" constructor
\item publication "[\@since M.m]"
\item expansion "[\@expanded M.m]",
  a constructor argument type can be expanded to a record type
\item requirement "[\@required M.m]",
  an optional record field can be made mandatory
\item deprecation "[\@deprecated M.m]",
  a record field can be marked as deprecated
\item deletion "[\@deleted M.m]"
\end{enumerate}

For instance, a fictional history of the "error_kind" might look like

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type error_kind =
  | Fatal of string [@since 2.0][@deleted 3.0]
  | Error of string [@since 1.0]
  | Warning of
      { contents:string; number:int; name:string }
      [@since 1.0] [@expanded 1.1]
  | Lint of string [@preview 1.1][@since 2.0]
\end{caml_example*}

Then the versioning policy merely describe the set of valid diagnostic data
types history.

\subsection{ss:sdiag-record-subtyping}{Record subtyping rule}

The subtyping rules for records are straightforward because we can
easily elide fields when coercing to a previous version.
This gives us three subtyping rules, a minor update may:

\begin{enumerate}
\item add new fields to a record type
\item promote an optional field to a required field
\item deprecate a field
\end{enumerate}

In particular, the last two rules only refine metadata information about the
field and do not affect coercion.


\subsection{ss:sdiag-variant-subtyping}{Variant subtyping rules}

The three subtyping rules for variants are more interesting. In this case,
a minor update may:

\begin{enumerate}
\item remove constructors from a sum type
\item expand the argument type of a variant constructor
\item introduce a new derived variant constructor
\end{enumerate}


The first rule merely states that if a diagnostic consumer knows how to handle a
constructor, it doesn't matter if the compiler actually emit this constructor or
not.

Unfortunately, this first rule is also insufficient by itself to allow any
meaningful evolution of the variants type.

The second rule alleviates this issue by allowing a minor update to change the
type of a constructor argument to a record type containing the original argument
inside a "content" field. Client relying on the original scheme can still read
this compatibility field.

For instance, a minor update might expand the constructor "Report_warning" in

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type example =
  | Report_warning of string [@since 1.0]
\end{caml_example*}

to

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type report_warning = { contents: string; name:string; number:int } [@@since 1.1]
type example =
  | Report_warning of report_warning [@since 1.0] [@expanded 1.1]
\end{caml_example*}


Finally, if a new constructor "c2" in version "v2" might be
approximated by an old constructor "c1" in "v1", then the compiler can
emit the new constructor as an optional refinement to old constructor.

For instance, if we want to add a "hint" report kind to the type

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type report_kind =
  | Report_error [@since 1.0]
  | Report_warning of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_warning_as_error of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_alert of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_alert_as_error of string [@since 1.0]
\end{caml_example*}

we can introduce the "Report_hint" as a derived constructor of "Report_warning"

\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
type report_kind =
  | Report_error [@since 1.0]
  | Report_warning of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_warning_as_error of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_alert of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_alert_as_error of string [@since 1.0]
  | Report_hint of string [@preview 1.1 Report_warning]
\end{caml_example*}

Then for as long as the  "Report_hint" constructor will be in a preview version,
it will be represented as
\begin{caml_eval}
type hreport_warning = { contents: string; next: report_kind option [@optional] }
type hrw = Report_warning of hreport_warning
\end{caml_eval}
\begin{caml_example*}{verbatim}
let contents = "warning_name" [@ellipsis] in
Report_warning { contents; next = Some (Report_hint contents) }
\end{caml_example*}
allowing consumers to easily ignore the existence of this constructor in preview
stage.

\subsection{ss:sdiag-major-update}{Backward-compatibility beyond major versions}

Going back to major version, when publish a new major version of the
diagnostics, we may

\begin{enumerate}
\item delete unused records and sum types
\item remove a deprecated field from a record type
\item add a new variant constructor to a variant type
\end{enumerate}

With those changes, enforcing compatibility between major versions is not always
possible, in particular for the variant constructor side. However, the compiler
does strive to provide some long term guarantees.

First, to avoid reuse of previous names with different meaning, the compiler
keeps a set of tombstones for deleted nominal types, record fields, and sum
constructors.

Second, in the record case, the specification of deleting only deprecated fields
means that the compiler supports a new field for at least two compiler versions.

Third, backward-compatibility for variant constructors is necessarily imperfect.
Thus reliable deserializers that expect to work across major versions would need
to parse variant sum as if they were always extensible.


\section{s:metadata-diag}{Compiler diagnostics}

The current schema of compiler diagnostics are hosted on in json-schema format
on \href{https://github.com/ocaml/ocaml}. Moreover, this section contains a more
human readable retranscription of those schema in the form of annotated
algebraic data types.

The current last version is:
\begin{itemize}
\item "1.0" for the "metadata" diagnostic
\item "1.0" for the "config" diagnostic
\item "0.1" for the compiler, toplevel and derived diagnostics
\end{itemize}

All those schema descriptions (and more) are generated using the
"ocamldiaginfo" tool available in the compiler source tree.

\subsection{ss:metadata-diag}{Metadata information}

The metadata field is present at the root of diagnostics directly
emitted by the compiler and contains both information about the version
of the scheme used to emit the diagnostics and if this specific diagnostic
is valid according to this scheme.

